file=Comms & PR/signal/custom/change-comms/SKILL.md · h=<!--\nCustom skill — built from scratch. NOT in the original catalog; added per §2 roster\nexpansion at operator sign-off for change-management comms specifically (reorg / layoff /\nmerger / major transitions) that need distinct discipline beyond routine internal-cadence.\n\nSource content:\n- Kotter, John P. Leading Change (Harvard Business School Press 1996; updated 2012).\n8-step change model. Academic + practitioner Harvard Business School professor.\n- Bridges, William & Susan. Managing Transitions: Making the Most of Change (1991,\n3rd ed 2009). Transition model — Ending / Neutral Zone / New Beginning. Distinct\nfrom "change" as external event.\n- Prosci ADKAR — Awareness / Desire / Knowledge / Ability / Reinforcement. Individual-\nchange methodology from Jeffrey Hiatt (Prosci Learning Center).\n- Patty McCord, Powerful (2018) — already cited across P&C identity + hire's Blind\nSpots §5 (underplays employment-law surface). Practitioner-operator per §8.9;\nreused here for the direct-but-honest layoff-comms voice.\n- Practitioner corpus: Fowler, Sinek, additional practitioner references for major-change\ncomms.\n\nNo genericization needed — change-management frameworks are public academic + practitioner\nwork. No VYON-branded content.\n\nRoute D per §8.2 (cited rubric + framework, no script).\n-->\nname: change-comms\ntype: custom\nstatus: built from scratch (added per §2 roster expansion beyond catalog's 2-skill floor)\nsources_referenced:\n- "Kotter, John P. (1996, updated 2012). Leading Change. Harvard Business School Press. Foundational 8-step change model. Named academic per §8.8 (Harvard Business School professor emeritus)."\n- "Bridges, William & Susan (1991, 3rd ed 2009). Managing Transitions: Making the Most of Change. Da Capo Lifelong Books. Transition model: Ending / Neutral Zone / New Beginning. Distinguishes external change from internal transition."\n- "Prosci ADKAR (Awareness / Desire / Knowledge / Ability / Reinforcement) — Jeffrey Hiatt / Prosci Learning Center. Individual-change methodology; institutional-adjacent practitioner framework."\n- "McCord, Patty (2018). Powerful. Silicon Guild. Direct-but-honest layoff-comms voice; already cited across P&C identity + hire's Blind Spots. §8.9 extract-once-use-twice."\n- "Additional practitioner corpus: Sinek's Start With Why for pre-change narrative; Duhigg's The Power of Habit for post-change reinforcement patterns."\nfulfills_catalog_entry: n/a (added per §2 roster expansion)\ngenericization_notes:\n- "No VYON-branded content — change-management frameworks are public academic + practitioner work."\nassigned_agent: signal (Comms & PR / Internal Comms)\nportable: true\ndate_added: 2026-07-31\ntier: 3\ndescription: Change-management comms for reorg / layoff / merger / major-transition events. Combines Kotter's 8-step change model + Bridges' transition model (Ending / Neutral Zone / New Beginning) + Prosci ADKAR (Awareness / Desire / Knowledge / Ability / Reinforcement). Distinct from routine internal-cadence — change events require narrative discipline that routine announcements don't. Trigger on "reorg announcement", "layoff comms", "merger comms", "major transition comms", "change management", "reorganize the team", or "how do we announce this change".\ntriggers:\n- reorg announcement\n- layoff comms\n- merger comms\n- major transition comms\n- change management\n- reorganize the team\n- how do we announce this change\n- transition plan for\n- pre-change communications\n- post-change reinforcement\n · in:=This skill covers **change-management communications** — the distinct discipline required\nwhen the internal event is a REORG, LAYOFF, MERGER, MAJOR RESTRUCTURE, or other MAJOR\nTRANSITION. Distinct from routine internal-cadence (weekly notes, monthly all-hands,\ndecision broadcasts) because change events combine legal-adjacent exposure + emotional\nweight + long timelines + high risk of misinterpretation.\n\nBuilt from scratch per §2 roster expansion. Sources: Kotter's 8-step change model\n(academic anchor), Bridges' transition model (distinguishes external change from\ninternal psychological transition), Prosci ADKAR (individual-change methodology), and\nMcCord's *Powerful* (direct-but-honest layoff voice, already cited across P&C).\n\n**Load-bearing scope boundary:** change-comms handles the COMMS discipline for\nchange events. It does NOT handle:\n- Legal-formalization of layoffs / restructures → operator + employment counsel per\nUniversal Principle 5 legal fence. WARN Act notices, protected-class impact analysis,\nseverance-agreement language, and international jurisdiction requirements are legal\nwork, not comms work.\n- Structural design of the reorg / workforce plan → hire's `workforce-planning` +\nmerit's `succession-planning`.\n- Individual crisis signals during change conversations → HARD BOUNDARY escalation per\nUniversal Principle 3.\n · p:=Prevents five failure modes that show up when major-change comms operate without\ndistinct discipline:\n\n1. **Change-as-directive** — leadership announces the change without pre-comms narrative\nor post-comms reinforcement; team receives it as arbitrary. Kotter's 8-step model\naddresses this: step 1 is "create a sense of urgency" (pre-comms), step 8 is\n"anchor changes in corporate culture" (post-comms).\n2. **Confused audience segmentation** — same layoff comms message goes to affected\nemployees + retained employees + external partners + customers. Each audience needs\ndistinct framing with distinct actionable content. Undifferentiated comms damages\ntrust in multiple directions simultaneously.\n3. **Bridges' Neutral Zone unmanaged** — announcement happens (Ending); new state\narrives eventually (New Beginning); but the middle period (Neutral Zone — high\nuncertainty, low productivity, high anxiety) gets ignored. Bridges argues most\nchange-management failures happen in the Neutral Zone, not at Ending or New Beginning.\n4. **Legal-formalization confused with comms** — comms person drafts language that\ncreates legal exposure (WARN Act violations, protected-class-adjacent phrasing,\nseverance-negotiation-adjacent statements). Legal fence per Universal Principle 5 —\nemployment counsel involved BEFORE any change comms ships.\n5. **Individual mental-health signals during change conversations** — layoffs and major\nreorgs surface distress. HARD BOUNDARY escalation per Universal Principle 3 (inherited).\n\nsignal uses this skill whenever an internal event qualifies as major-change (reorg /\nlayoff / merger / major restructure) — distinct from the routine cadence that\n`internal-cadence` (sibling) owns.\n · wu:=Trigger on:\n\n- "Reorg announcement" / "reorganize the team" / "team restructure"\n- "Layoff comms" / "layoff announcement" / "reduction in force" / "RIF"\n- "Merger comms" / "acquisition comms" / "M&A announcement"\n- "Major transition comms" / "org change"\n- "Change management" (in an internal-comms context)\n- "How do we announce this change" (when the change qualifies as major — vs routine\ndecision broadcast which routes to `internal-cadence`)\n- "Pre-change communications" / "post-change reinforcement"\n- "Transition plan for [scope]"\n- Handoff from hire's `workforce-planning` when a structural change is decided and\nneeds comms discipline\n\nDo NOT use for:\n\n- **Routine decision broadcasts / weekly cadence / all-hands** → sibling\n`internal-cadence` skill.\n- **Format templates** (3P / newsletter / FAQ / general) → sibling marketplace\n`internal-comms` skill.\n- **External-facing change comms** (press coverage of the reorg, investor\ndisclosure of the merger) → herald's `press-kit` + `media-relations` + beacon's\n`crisis-comms` + `investor-cadence` + operator + securities counsel.\n- **Legal formalization of the change** (WARN Act notices, severance agreements,\nprotected-class impact analysis) → operator + employment counsel per Universal\nPrinciple 5 legal fence. Comms discipline does NOT substitute for legal work.\n- **Structural design of the change itself** (org chart, headcount decisions, reporting\nlines) → hire's `workforce-planning` + merit's `succession-planning`.\n- **Individual mental-health signals during change conversations** → HARD BOUNDARY\nescalation per Universal Principle 3.\n · st:/ Protocol=The change-comms workflow combines three frameworks:\n\n```\nKOTTER 8-STEP CHANGE MODEL (organizational-change process)\n1. Create a sense of urgency          (pre-comms narrative)\n2. Build a guiding coalition          (leadership alignment)\n3. Form a strategic vision            (what the new state looks like)\n4. Enlist a volunteer army            (early adopters + advocates)\n5. Enable action by removing barriers (unblock the transition)\n6. Generate short-term wins           (visible progress)\n7. Sustain acceleration               (build on wins)\n8. Institute change                   (anchor in culture)\n\nBRIDGES TRANSITION MODEL (individual-psychological transition)\nPhase 1: ENDING          — letting go of the old (grief; loss; identity change)\nPhase 2: NEUTRAL ZONE    — the messy middle (uncertainty; ambiguity; low productivity)\nPhase 3: NEW BEGINNING   — embracing the new (energy; commitment; identity formation)\n\nPROSCI ADKAR (individual-change milestones — sequential)\nA: AWARENESS      — of the need for change\nD: DESIRE         — to support the change (voluntary participation)\nK: KNOWLEDGE      — of how to change (skills + information)\nA: ABILITY        — to demonstrate new skills + behaviors\nR: REINFORCEMENT  — to sustain the change\n\nCHANGE-COMMS SEQUENCE (this skill's operational workflow):\n1. Confirm scope + change type (reorg / layoff / merger / transition)\n2. Confirm legal-fence status  — employment counsel involved BEFORE comms ships\n3. Segment audiences           — affected / retained / customers / external\n4. Draft pre-change narrative  — Kotter step 1 + Sinek Start-With-Why + Bridges Ending\n5. Draft announcement          — audience-specific; honest WHY; explicit\nWHAT-CHANGES per audience\n6. Design Neutral Zone comms   — high-cadence updates during the messy middle\n(Bridges' most-often-neglected phase)\n7. Design reinforcement comms  — Kotter step 8 + ADKAR reinforcement; post-change\ninstitutional-anchoring\n8. Archive + close-the-loop    — per internal-cadence archive discipline\n```\n · ### Phase 1 — Confirm scope + change type=Confirm the change qualifies as major-change requiring this skill's discipline (vs\nroutine decision broadcast which routes to `internal-cadence`):\n\n- **Reorg** — change in reporting lines, team structure, function boundaries, or\nleadership responsibilities affecting >2-3 people.\n- **Layoff / RIF** — involuntary departures beyond individual performance-management.\n- **Merger / acquisition** — M&A affecting company structure, ownership, or\nemployment.\n- **Major transition** — office relocation, business-model shift, product-line\ndiscontinuation, or any change that substantially changes work for a meaningful\ncohort.\n\nIf the change doesn't qualify, route to `internal-cadence`'s decision-broadcast structure\n(smaller, faster, less discipline overhead).\n · ### Phase 2 — Confirm legal-fence status BEFORE any drafting=**Universal Principle 5 legal fence LOAD-BEARING for change-comms.** Employment counsel\nmust be involved BEFORE any change-comms ships. Specifically:\n\n- **Layoffs / RIF** — WARN Act (US federal + state-specific) notification requirements;\nprotected-class impact analysis; severance-agreement language; international\njurisdiction requirements (varying notice periods, works-council consultation\nrequirements in EU, etc.).\n- **Reorg with role eliminations** — same as layoff subset.\n- **Merger / acquisition** — securities-law disclosure timing (SEC Reg FD for public\ncompanies), employment-contract implications for affected employees, works-council\nrequirements.\n- **Major transition** — location-specific employment-law implications (e.g., office\nrelocation triggering constructive-dismissal claims).\n\nIf employment counsel is NOT involved, HOLD the comms drafting. Route to operator to\nengage counsel before proceeding.\n · ### Phase 3 — Segment audiences=Change comms almost never uses a single message. Standard audience segments:\n\n- **Affected employees** (layoff targets, reorged-out roles) — first + separate + most\ncompassionate + most operational (severance + timing + next-steps clarity).\n- **Directly affected employees** (retained but role-changed by the reorg) — WHAT this\nchanges for you specifically + support during Neutral Zone.\n- **Retained employees** (not directly affected but part of same team / function) —\nwhat changes for the team + reassurance about future stability + honest information\nabout why colleagues are leaving.\n- **Adjacent teams** (downstream / upstream teams affected by the reorg) — how\nhandoffs change + who to contact for what.\n- **Customers / partners** — external-facing narrative (routes to herald's `media-relations`\n+ `press-kit` for external drafting; signal coordinates for internal-external\nconsistency).\n- **Board / investors** — material-info disclosure (routes to beacon's `investor-cadence`\n+ operator + securities counsel).\n\nEach segment gets DISTINCT framing. Same message to all segments = misinterpretation in\nmultiple directions.\n · ### Phase 4 — Draft pre-change narrative (Kotter step 1 + Bridges Ending)=**Weeks-to-days BEFORE announcement**, if the change is planned + non-secret:\n\n- **Sense of urgency** (Kotter step 1) — why the org needs to change now. Business\ndriver, market context, strategic pivot. Honest. Not "we're growing so fast we need\nto restructure" (which is often false); instead the real reason.\n- **Guiding coalition** (Kotter step 2) — leadership alignment on the change. Change\ncomms fail when leadership publicly aligns but privately dissents; alignment must be\ngenuine before comms ships.\n- **Strategic vision** (Kotter step 3) — what the new state looks like. Concrete enough\nthat people can picture their role in it.\n\nFor layoffs specifically, the pre-change narrative is often limited (surprise\nannouncement is required for legal + security reasons — WARN Act timing rules,\ninsider-trading concerns for public companies). Legal-fence phase 2 determines what\npre-comms is permitted.\n · ### Phase 5 — Draft announcement (audience-specific + honest WHY + WHAT-CHANGES)=Per Phase 3 audience segments, draft distinct announcements:\n\n**Structure per announcement (inherits from `internal-cadence` decision-broadcast\nstructure but expanded):**\n\n- **WHAT changed** — concrete + specific.\n- **WHY it changed** — honest + non-euphemistic. Corporate euphemism erodes trust\nmore than the underlying news does (McCord discipline; already anchored in Scott\nidentity + hire principles).\n- **WHAT this changes for YOU** — per-audience specific.\n- **TIMING** — dates, sequences, milestones.\n- **RESOURCES** — where to get more information, who to contact, EAP availability\nfor affected employees, transition support.\n- **NEXT COMMUNICATION** — when the next update will come + through what channel.\n\nFor layoff communications specifically, McCord's discipline: **direct, plain English,\nadult presumption, no euphemism, treat affected people with respect + as adults.** The\nMcCord voice is inherited from Scott identity via department-leader inheritance +\nalready-anchored across P&C.\n · ### Phase 6 — Design Neutral Zone comms (Bridges' most-neglected phase)=**The Neutral Zone is where most change-management fails.** Between announcement\n(Ending) and stable new state (New Beginning), teams experience:\n\n- High uncertainty about role, priorities, reporting relationships\n- Low productivity (measurable; Bridges cites 30-50% temporary decline)\n- High anxiety and turnover risk\n- Rumor generation to fill information gaps\n\n**High-cadence updates DURING the Neutral Zone** — much more frequent than routine\ninternal-cadence:\n\n- Weekly (minimum) written updates from change leadership on progress + open questions\n+ what's still uncertain\n- Regular office-hours or Q&A forums where team can ask questions\n- Explicit acknowledgment of the difficulty ("this is a hard time; we know") —\nperformative acknowledgment fails; genuine acknowledgment builds trust\n- Named point-of-contact for questions per affected group\n\nSkipping Neutral Zone comms = the change fails in practice even if the org-chart-level\nchange succeeds.\n · ### Phase 7 — Design reinforcement comms (Kotter step 8 + ADKAR Reinforcement)=**Weeks-to-months AFTER the New Beginning starts**, reinforce the change through:\n\n- **Short-term wins broadcast** (Kotter step 6) — visible progress in the new state.\n- **Institutional anchoring** (Kotter step 8) — new processes, new team names, new\nmeeting cadences, new success metrics. Comms references the new state as normal;\nstops referring to the old state as reference point.\n- **Reinforcement per ADKAR** — celebrate examples of desired new behavior; correct\nregression to old behavior; make the new state the default.\n\nWithout reinforcement, teams drift back to old behaviors within 6-12 months even after\napparently-successful change.\n · ### Phase 8 — Archive + close-the-loop (per internal-cadence discipline)=- All change-comms artifacts to searchable archive (per `internal-cadence` Phase 5\narchive discipline).\n- Close-the-loop discipline continues during + after change: visible actions from\nprevious cycle's feedback communicated before next update (per `internal-cadence`\nPhase 6 minimum-viable-action rule).\n- Post-change retrospective: what worked / what didn't / lessons for next change event.\n · of:=Each invocation produces one or more of:\n\n- **Change-scope confirmation memo** — is this major-change requiring change-comms\ndiscipline, or routine decision routing to `internal-cadence`?\n- **Legal-fence status check** — employment counsel involvement confirmed for the change\ntype before drafting proceeds.\n- **Audience segmentation plan** — per Phase 3.\n- **Pre-change narrative brief** — Kotter step 1-3 (urgency + coalition + vision) where\npre-comms is permitted.\n- **Announcement drafts** (audience-specific) — WHAT / WHY / WHAT-CHANGES-FOR-YOU /\nTIMING / RESOURCES / NEXT COMMUNICATION.\n- **Neutral Zone comms plan** — cadence + channels + named-point-of-contact per group +\nQ&A forum design.\n- **Reinforcement plan** — Kotter step 8 + ADKAR reinforcement.\n- **Post-change retrospective** — what worked + what didn't + lessons for next change.\n · pr:=1. **Legal fence BEFORE comms drafting.** Universal Principle 5 (inherited). Employment\ncounsel involved before any change-comms ships. WARN Act, protected-class impact,\nseverance language, international requirements are LEGAL work; do not draft comms\nlanguage that creates legal exposure.\n2. **Audience segmentation is mandatory.** Same message to all audiences = misinterpretation\nin multiple directions. Affected / retained / adjacent / external / board — each\ndistinct.\n3. **Honest WHY, no corporate euphemism.** Inherited from `internal-cadence` Principle\n3; sharpened here — "headwinds," "efficiency measures," "strategic reallocation,"\n"personnel adjustments" during a layoff erode trust more than the underlying news\ndoes. McCord discipline (inherited via Scott identity + P&C).\n4. **Neutral Zone comms is the highest-leverage phase.** Most change failures happen\nbetween announcement and new stable state (Bridges). High-cadence updates during\nthe Neutral Zone are non-optional — skipping them = the change fails even if the\nstructural change succeeds.\n5. **Reinforcement lasts weeks-to-months, not days.** Kotter step 8 + ADKAR Reinforcement\nsustain the change. Without reinforcement, teams drift back to old behaviors within\n6-12 months.\n6. **Individual mental-health signals during change conversations escalate immediately.**\nUniversal Principle 3 (inherited). Change events surface distress; HARD BOUNDARY to\nmanager + HR Ops + EAP.\n7. **Aggregate-only at publication surface.** Universal Principle 7 (inherited). Change\ncomms may name individual leadership (attribution for decisions) but never publish\nindividual perf data, individual severance details, or individual demographic data.\n8. **Change-comms does NOT substitute for structural or legal work.** Structural design\n→ hire's workforce-planning + merit's succession-planning. Legal formalization →\noperator + employment counsel. This skill covers COMMS discipline only.\n9. **§0.6 flag.** Kotter 8-step + Bridges 3-phase + Prosci ADKAR are Tier B (canonical\npublished frameworks cited but not book-page-cited from `Agents/_books/`). Downgrade\nto Tier A when Kotter 2012 + Bridges 2009 + Prosci institutional materials are\nplaced and a `Shared OS/logical/change_comms.md` Route-D asset is built per §8.9.\n · f:=- **Legal counsel not involved.** HOLD drafting. Route to operator to engage counsel.\nComms language that creates legal exposure is worse than delayed comms.\n- **Change scope unclear** (is this major-change or routine?). Ask requester + err on\nthe side of change-comms discipline for anything affecting >2-3 people substantially.\nOver-formalizing change comms is cheap; under-formalizing burns trust.\n- **Pre-change narrative not permitted** (surprise required for legal / security /\ninsider-trading reasons). Accept the constraint; move directly to Phase 5 (announcement)\n+ Phase 6 (Neutral Zone comms). Note the lack of pre-comms in the change retrospective.\n- **Audience segmentation collapses** (operator wants "one message for everyone" for\nspeed). Push back per Principle 2 — undifferentiated change comms damages trust in\nmultiple directions. If operator insists, ship with explicit note that segmentation\nwas skipped and this is expected to reduce message effectiveness.\n- **Neutral Zone comms plan requires more resource than available.** Escalate — recommend\nreducing the change scope OR extending the timeline OR investing in additional\nchange-comms resource. Skipping Neutral Zone comms = the change fails.\n- **Euphemism creeping in from executive drafts.** Push back per Principle 3 — provide\ndirect plain-English alternatives + explain the trust cost of euphemism.\n- **Individual mental-health signal during change conversation.** STOP. Route per\nUniversal Principle 3 to manager + HR Ops + EAP.\n- **Change comms requested for external audiences** (customers / partners / press). Route\nto herald's `press-kit` + `media-relations` for external drafting; signal coordinates\nfor internal-external consistency but does NOT draft external-facing change comms.\n- **Material NPI in change announcement** (M&A, financial restatement, executive\ndeparture). Route to beacon + operator + securities counsel BEFORE internal broadcast.\nInternal announcement timing coordinates with external disclosure timing per SEC Reg\nFD for public companies.\n · tb=\| Hands off to / from \| For \| Direction \| · tb=\|---\|---\|---\| · tb=\| `internal-cadence` (custom, signal — sibling) \| Routine decision broadcasts / weekly cadence / all-hands cadence discipline; archive discipline \| Downstream — change-comms uses internal-cadence's archive discipline; upstream — routine decisions route TO internal-cadence, change events route AWAY from it \| · tb=\| `internal-comms` (marketplace, signal — sibling) \| Format templates (3P / newsletter / FAQ / general) when change-comms uses those formats \| Coordination \| · tb=\| `hire` + `workforce-planning` (custom, hire — P&C) \| Structural design of reorg / headcount change (workforce-planning owns structure; change-comms owns the comms) \| Upstream — workforce-planning provides the structural change; change-comms announces it \| · tb=\| `succession-planning` (custom, merit — P&C) \| Succession-adjacent changes (executive departure, leadership transition) \| Coordination \| · tb=\| `feedback-methods` (custom, merit — P&C) \| Delivery discipline for individual 1:1 conversations that accompany the aggregate change comms (SBI + Radical Candor) \| Downstream — for the individual manager-to-directs conversations that accompany the aggregate announcement \| · tb=\| `motivation-map` + `wellbeing-monitoring` (custom, maslow — P&C) \| Aggregate motivation/wellbeing signals during and after change (Neutral Zone monitoring); recovery-signal tracking post-change \| Cross-department coordination \| · tb=\| `press-kit` + `media-relations` (custom, herald — Comms & PR sibling) \| External-facing change comms (press coverage of the reorg, board-level narrative) \| Coordination — signal handles internal; herald handles external; consistency between the two mandatory \| · tb=\| `crisis-comms` (custom, beacon — Comms & PR sibling) \| If the change becomes a crisis (leaked news, hostile press coverage, unexpected market reaction) \| Escalation — change-comms handles routine change; crisis-comms handles the crisis dimension \| · tb=\| `investor-cadence` + `data-room-discipline` (custom, beacon — sibling) \| Material-info fence for M&A / restructure comms; investor-facing narrative \| Coordination + escalation \| · tb=\| `board` (Governance) \| Governance approval for major change; material NPI approval \| Escalation \| · tb=\| Operator + employment counsel \| LOAD-BEARING legal fence — WARN Act, protected-class impact, severance language, international requirements \| Escalation BEFORE drafting (Phase 2) \| · tb=\| Operator + securities counsel \| SEC Reg FD + material-info disclosure timing for public companies \| Escalation \| · tb=\| Manager + HR Ops + EAP \| Individual mental-health signal during change conversation — HARD BOUNDARY \| Escalation — Universal Principle 3 \| · tb=\| `Shared OS: verification-before-completion` \| Evidence gate on every change-comms artifact before shipping \| Cross-cutting \| · r(/:References (public / verifiable)=- [Kotter, John P. — 8-Step Change Model overview (Kotter Inc.)](https://www.kotterinc.com/methodology/8-steps/)\n- [Bridges Transition Model — William Bridges Associates](https://wmbridges.com/what-is-transition/)\n- [Prosci ADKAR Model overview](https://www.prosci.com/methodology/adkar)\n- [McCord, Patty — Powerful book site](https://pattymccord.com/)\n- [Kotter (2012). Accelerate — HBR excerpt](https://hbr.org/2012/11/accelerate)\n- [Prosci — Change Management institutional materials](https://www.prosci.com/resources/articles)\n